WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.846
Welcome to Nabulines Academy.

2
00:00:02.194 --> 00:00:03.936
You've got your Claude account, you've

3
00:00:03.971 --> 00:00:05.352
seen Artifacts in action.

4
00:00:05.817 --> 00:00:07.152
Now let's talk about the skill that

5
00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:09.636
separates beginners from builders: how to

6
00:00:09.706 --> 00:00:11.099
actually talk to AI.

7
00:00:11.738 --> 00:00:13.746
This isn't about magic words or tricks.

8
00:00:13.967 --> 00:00:15.859
It's about being clear, specific, and

9
00:00:15.917 --> 00:00:17.612
giving Claude what it needs to help you.

10
00:00:18.251 --> 00:00:19.667
By the end of this lesson, you'll have

11
00:00:19.714 --> 00:00:21.467
three simple patterns you can use every

12
00:00:21.513 --> 00:00:23.232
single time you ask Claude for something.

13
00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:24.335
Let's go.

14
00:00:24.335 --> 00:00:26.332
First, let's talk about what a prompt

15
00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:27.365
actually is.

16
00:00:27.772 --> 00:00:29.559
A prompt is just the message you send to

17
00:00:29.606 --> 00:00:32.149
Claude — the question, the request, the

18
00:00:32.195 --> 00:00:32.845
instruction.

19
00:00:33.484 --> 00:00:35.306
Think of it like this: Claude is

20
00:00:35.353 --> 00:00:37.419
incredibly smart, but it can't read your

21
00:00:37.466 --> 00:00:37.895
mind.

22
00:00:38.534 --> 00:00:40.972
If you say "make me a website," Claude has

23
00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:41.611
to guess.

24
00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:43.283
What kind of website?

25
00:00:43.341 --> 00:00:43.933
For who?

26
00:00:44.188 --> 00:00:45.187
What should it look like?

27
00:00:45.477 --> 00:00:46.870
The more you guess, the more you get

28
00:00:46.917 --> 00:00:47.776
something random.

29
00:00:48.414 --> 00:00:50.168
But if you're specific — if you say

30
00:00:50.214 --> 00:00:52.164
exactly what you want — Claude can build

31
00:00:52.222 --> 00:00:53.163
exactly that.

32
00:00:53.801 --> 00:00:55.427
So prompting is really just being a good

33
00:00:55.462 --> 00:00:56.158
communicator.

34
00:00:56.414 --> 00:00:57.389
You're not coding yet.

35
00:00:57.679 --> 00:00:59.072
You're describing what you need in plain

36
00:00:59.119 --> 00:00:59.537
English.

37
00:00:59.723 --> 00:01:01.325
And the better you describe it, the better

38
00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.091
the result.

39
00:01:02.091 --> 00:01:03.519
Here are the three patterns that will

40
00:01:03.577 --> 00:01:04.947
change everything for you.

41
00:01:05.493 --> 00:01:08.709
Pattern one: Context, Goal, Constraints.

42
00:01:09.347 --> 00:01:11.158
Always tell Claude three things.

43
00:01:11.797 --> 00:01:14.131
Context: what's the situation?

44
00:01:14.351 --> 00:01:15.292
Who is this for?

45
00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:17.312
Goal: what do you want?

46
00:01:17.857 --> 00:01:18.728
Be specific.

47
00:01:19.471 --> 00:01:22.641
Constraints: any limits or requirements?

48
00:01:23.279 --> 00:01:26.379
Like "keep it under 100 words" or "use

49
00:01:26.426 --> 00:01:30.466
only HTML and CSS." Pattern two: Show,

50
00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:31.418
don't just tell.

51
00:01:32.289 --> 00:01:33.949
If you want something that looks a certain

52
00:01:34.007 --> 00:01:35.377
way, describe it visually.

53
00:01:36.248 --> 00:01:38.454
Or better yet, paste an example and say

54
00:01:38.558 --> 00:01:41.182
"like this, but for my topic." Pattern

55
00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:42.645
three: Iterate.

56
00:01:42.831 --> 00:01:44.630
Your first prompt won't be perfect.

57
00:01:44.816 --> 00:01:45.559
That's fine.

58
00:01:45.849 --> 00:01:47.022
Ask Claude to refine it.

59
00:01:47.312 --> 00:01:49.925
Say "make the colors softer" or "add a

60
00:01:49.983 --> 00:01:51.689
contact form." Treat it like a

61
00:01:51.747 --> 00:01:54.197
conversation, not a one-shot command.

62
00:01:54.742 --> 00:01:56.670
These three patterns work for everything —

63
00:01:57.018 --> 00:01:59.491
a landing page, a script, a lesson plan,

64
00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:00.373
anything.

65
00:02:00.373 --> 00:02:01.615
Let's see what this looks like in

66
00:02:01.673 --> 00:02:02.207
practice.

67
00:02:02.498 --> 00:02:04.715
Here's a vague prompt: "Make me a

68
00:02:04.773 --> 00:02:07.014
website." Claude will do something, but

69
00:02:07.049 --> 00:02:08.697
it's a coin flip whether it's useful.

70
00:02:09.104 --> 00:02:10.892
Now watch what happens when we apply the

71
00:02:10.938 --> 00:02:11.530
patterns.

72
00:02:12.169 --> 00:02:14.432
Context: I'm a freelance photographer.

73
00:02:15.071 --> 00:02:17.381
Goal: I need a simple portfolio page to

74
00:02:17.439 --> 00:02:18.136
show my work.

75
00:02:18.775 --> 00:02:21.770
Constraints: One page, clean design, a

76
00:02:21.828 --> 00:02:23.976
grid of six photos, and a contact button

77
00:02:24.011 --> 00:02:24.614
at the bottom.

78
00:02:25.253 --> 00:02:27.285
Now Claude knows exactly what to build.

79
00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:30.559
Let's add pattern two — show, don't tell.

80
00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:33.334
I'll say: "Make it look modern and

81
00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:35.888
minimal, like Apple's product pages — lots

82
00:02:35.923 --> 00:02:38.860
of white space, big images." Now Claude

83
00:02:38.883 --> 00:02:40.091
has a visual reference.

84
00:02:40.637 --> 00:02:43.284
And pattern three: after Claude builds it,

85
00:02:43.423 --> 00:02:45.245
I'll say "Can you make the photo grid

86
00:02:45.292 --> 00:02:48.461
three columns instead of two?" or "Add my

87
00:02:48.543 --> 00:02:50.075
Instagram link next to the contact

88
00:02:50.122 --> 00:02:52.978
button." See how we're steering the result

89
00:02:53.036 --> 00:02:53.848
step by step?

90
00:02:54.313 --> 00:02:55.729
That's the art of prompting.

91
00:02:55.729 --> 00:02:57.296
Here's the mistake everyone makes at

92
00:02:57.354 --> 00:02:59.734
first: they think prompting is about

93
00:02:59.816 --> 00:03:01.801
finding the perfect magic sentence.

94
00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:03.078
It's not.

95
00:03:03.484 --> 00:03:05.063
It's about clarity and iteration.

96
00:03:05.284 --> 00:03:06.654
Your first try will be rough.

97
00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:07.838
That's normal.

98
00:03:08.384 --> 00:03:10.079
You'll ask Claude for something, look at

99
00:03:10.102 --> 00:03:11.832
the result, and realize you forgot to

100
00:03:11.879 --> 00:03:13.875
mention the color scheme or the font size.

101
00:03:14.131 --> 00:03:16.430
That's fine — just tell Claude to fix it.

102
00:03:16.778 --> 00:03:18.415
The best prompters aren't the ones who

103
00:03:18.473 --> 00:03:20.272
write perfect prompts on the first try.

104
00:03:20.736 --> 00:03:22.165
They're the ones who know how to steer the

105
00:03:22.199 --> 00:03:23.000
conversation.

106
00:03:23.291 --> 00:03:25.322
They say "a little bigger," "more

107
00:03:25.357 --> 00:03:27.041
playful," "add a section for

108
00:03:27.087 --> 00:03:29.583
testimonials." They treat Claude like a

109
00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:31.302
collaborator, not a vending machine.

110
00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:34.111
And that's the mindset shift: you're not

111
00:03:34.134 --> 00:03:35.759
trying to trick the AI into doing what you

112
00:03:35.817 --> 00:03:36.108
want.

113
00:03:36.398 --> 00:03:37.977
You're having a conversation with a really

114
00:03:38.023 --> 00:03:39.428
smart assistant who needs clear

115
00:03:39.475 --> 00:03:40.357
instructions.

116
00:03:40.357 --> 00:03:42.633
One more thing before we wrap: always ask

117
00:03:42.679 --> 00:03:43.619
Claude to explain.

118
00:03:44.165 --> 00:03:45.628
If you don't understand something in the

119
00:03:45.674 --> 00:03:48.229
code it wrote, just say "Explain this line

120
00:03:48.252 --> 00:03:50.806
to me like I'm five." Claude will break it

121
00:03:50.852 --> 00:03:51.247
down.

122
00:03:51.712 --> 00:03:53.581
If something doesn't work, say "This

123
00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:56.727
button isn't clickable — why?" Claude will

124
00:03:56.773 --> 00:03:57.760
debug it with you.

125
00:03:58.051 --> 00:03:59.699
You're not expected to know everything.

126
00:04:00.047 --> 00:04:01.835
You're expected to ask good questions.

127
00:04:02.126 --> 00:04:03.914
And that's a skill you'll use forever —

128
00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.155
not just with AI, but with every tool,

129
00:04:06.317 --> 00:04:08.105
every person, every project.

130
00:04:08.569 --> 00:04:10.415
Asking clear questions is how you learn

131
00:04:10.473 --> 00:04:11.100
fast.

132
00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.062
Alright, you now know the three patterns

133
00:04:13.085 --> 00:04:14.583
that will make every conversation with

134
00:04:14.629 --> 00:04:17.799
Claude better: Context, Goal, Constraints.

135
00:04:18.438 --> 00:04:19.831
Show, don't just tell.

136
00:04:20.841 --> 00:04:23.035
And Iterate — refine as you go.

137
00:04:23.581 --> 00:04:25.612
Your exercise is to take the vague prompt

138
00:04:25.833 --> 00:04:27.993
"make me a website" and rewrite it using

139
00:04:28.039 --> 00:04:28.887
these patterns.

140
00:04:29.432 --> 00:04:30.233
Be specific.

141
00:04:30.524 --> 00:04:31.476
Add context.

142
00:04:31.882 --> 00:04:32.892
Add constraints.

143
00:04:33.298 --> 00:04:34.808
Make it something Claude can actually

144
00:04:34.877 --> 00:04:35.318
build.

145
00:04:35.957 --> 00:04:37.513
Do that, and you're ready for the next

146
00:04:37.571 --> 00:04:39.428
lesson, where we'll use these prompting

147
00:04:39.475 --> 00:04:41.158
skills to build your first real page in

148
00:04:41.205 --> 00:04:41.832
Artifacts.

149
00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:43.283
See you there.
